          "Social structures and demographics — social stratification: hierarchical arrangement of society into layers based on unequal distribution of resources, power, and prestige; three classical theories: (1) Marx: binary class division — bourgeoisie (own means of production, extract surplus value) vs proletariat (sell labor power); class consciousness when workers recognize their shared interests; false consciousness when they accept bourgeois ideology that naturalizes exploitation; (2) Weber: multidimensional stratification — class (economic), status (social prestige, lifestyle, honor), and party/power (political influence) as analytically separate and independently variable dimensions; (3) Davis-Moore functionalist thesis: stratification allocates talent to functionally important positions via differential rewards — widely criticized for circular reasoning and ignoring structural barriers to entry; social class indicators: income (earnings flow), wealth (net assets — home equity, investments; far more unequally distributed than income), education, and occupational prestige; SES (socioeconomic status) is a composite of these; social mobility: vertical (upward or downward) vs horizontal (same level, different position); intergenerational (children's position vs parents') vs intragenerational (within a lifetime); absolute mobility (general rise as economy grows) vs relative mobility (rank changes — more structural); meritocracy thesis (talent + effort → success) challenged by structural barriers (race, class, gender); U.S. intergenerational earnings elasticity ≈ 0.45 — lower mobility than most peer nations; poverty: absolute poverty (below threshold for basic needs — U.S. federal poverty line) vs relative poverty (below fraction of median income); poverty traps: geographic (concentrated disadvantage, under-resourced schools), institutional (criminal record → employment barriers), behavioral; Gini coefficient (0=perfect equality, 1=perfect inequality; U.S. ≈ 0.48 pre-redistribution, ≈ 0.39 post-transfers); race and ethnicity: race as social construct — genetic variation between socially defined racial groups is minimal; race is imposed by social/political systems and has real social consequences despite lacking biological basis; ethnicity: shared cultural practices, language, ancestry, religion, and history; racialization: process by which racial meaning attaches to previously unracialized groups or practices; prejudice (negative attitude toward a group) vs discrimination (unequal treatment based on group membership); individual discrimination vs institutional/structural discrimination (policies that systematically disadvantage groups regardless of individual intent — e.g., redlining, racially disparate sentencing); implicit bias: automatic, unconscious associations measured by IAT; stereotype threat (Steele & Aronson): awareness of a negative stereotype about one's group impairs performance in that domain; intersectionality (Kimberlé Crenshaw): overlapping social identities (race, class, gender, sexuality, disability, immigration status) interact to produce unique forms of privilege and oppression — cannot be understood by examining any single axis alone; gender: sex (biological — chromosomes, gonads, hormones) vs gender (social/psychological — roles, identities, expression); gender stratification: unequal distribution of rewards by gender; occupational segregation (horizontal: concentration in certain fields; vertical: glass ceiling); gender wage gap (~82 cents/$1 in U.S.); age stratification and ageism; social determinants of health (SDOH): income, education, employment, housing, food security (food deserts), neighborhood safety, and social support networks — graded SES health gradient (Whitehall studies), each SES step confers better health outcomes via material conditions, psychosocial stress (chronic stress, allostatic load), and health behaviors; racial health disparities: driven by structural racism, residential segregation, chronic stress (weathering hypothesis — Geronimus: premature aging from cumulative stress burden), and unequal care access; healthy immigrant effect: recent immigrants often have better health than U.S.-born counterparts despite lower SES — selective migration plus cultural protective factors, but health deteriorates with acculturation; immigration status and access to healthcare, education, and social services; sick role (Talcott Parsons): (1) exempt from normal obligations, (2) not blamed for illness, (3) obligated to try to get well, (4) obligated to seek medical care — criticized for ignoring chronic illness and structural barriers; medicalization: defining and treating non-medical problems as medical (ADHD, alcoholism, childbirth, menopause); social institutions: organized systems fulfilling fundamental social needs — family (socialization, care), education (knowledge transmission and credentialing but also reproduces inequality via hidden curriculum, tracking, and differential funding), religion (cohesion and meaning), government/political institutions, medicine (gatekeeping sick role and social control of deviance); demographic transition model: Stage 1 (high birth + high death), Stage 2 (falling death rates — rapid population growth), Stage 3 (falling birth rates), Stage 4 (low birth + low death — post-industrial); a 4–6 question MCAT Psych/Soc Section topic tested via: matching a scenario to a stratification theory (Marx/Weber/functionalist), applying intersectionality to a research vignette, identifying sick role criteria in a patient case, distinguishing individual from structural discrimination, explaining the healthy immigrant effect, and recognizing social determinants affecting a clinical case — previously a bare label in the MCAT Psych/Soc pack",
