          "Evidence and reasoning — strengthen, weaken, undermine arguments: argument anatomy — every CARS argument has three layers: (1) conclusion (the main claim the author wants you to accept, often stated in evaluative language: 'therefore', 'thus', 'it follows that', 'this suggests', 'clearly'), (2) premises (the evidence or facts offered to support the conclusion — data, expert testimony, historical examples, anecdotes, logical inference from accepted facts), and (3) assumptions (unstated premises the argument requires to be true — the logical 'bridge' between the evidence and conclusion; attacking an assumption is the most efficient way to weaken an argument); question-stem recognition — 'most strengthens the argument' asks for evidence that makes the conclusion more probable; 'most weakens/undermines' asks for evidence that makes the conclusion less probable or attacks a required assumption; 'most undermines' specifically targets assumptions or evidence — not peripheral details; 'most supports' asks which answer is most consistent with the passage's claims (not necessarily the conclusion); strengthen logic: correct answer adds a new fact that closes an evidential gap or addresses a counter-argument already in the passage; weaken/undermine logic: correct answer supplies a counter-example, reveals a methodological flaw (sampling bias, confound, failure to control for an alternative cause), or shows that an unstated assumption is false; correlation vs. causation — the single most-tested reasoning flaw in MCAT CARS: a passage that observes A and B co-occur and concludes A causes B is weakened by any choice that names an alternative cause of B, shows B also occurs without A, or reveals the relationship is reverse-causal (B causes A); hasty generalization — conclusion drawn from an unrepresentative, too-small, or otherwise limited sample; the 'sample to population' assumption is a frequent target; false analogy — conclusion transferred from one domain assumes both contexts are relevantly similar (undermine by identifying a morally or causally relevant difference); argument from authority — appeals to expert consensus without direct evidence; weakened by showing the experts have a conflict of interest, disagree internally, or are outside their domain of expertise; scope shift — premises talk about one group or context, conclusion claims something about a broader or different group; answer choice traps — (i) out-of-scope: mentions entities, time periods, or concepts never discussed in the passage; (ii) extreme language (always, never, all, none) — requires a universal claim the passage cannot support; (iii) reversal: does the opposite of what the question asks (strengthens when you need to weaken); (iv) too-weak: vaguely related but does not actually affect the probability of the conclusion; (v) conditional vs. unconditional: introduces a conditional that only sometimes applies; strategy — before reading answer choices, articulate in one sentence what assumption the argument relies on; the correct weakener will attack that assumption; the correct strengthener will support it or rule out a common counter-argument; a 4–6 question MCAT CARS skill tested in every CARS passage (≈53% of all CARS questions involve reasoning rather than retrieval); explicitly covered in 'foundation of comprehension', 'reasoning within the text', and 'reasoning beyond the text' categories on the AAMC score report",
