          "Recursion (AP CSA Unit 10) — a method that calls itself to solve a problem by reducing it to a smaller instance of the same problem; every correct recursive method requires (1) a base case — a condition that stops the recursion and returns a value directly (e.g., n == 0 or n == 1), without which the chain of calls never terminates and throws StackOverflowError; (2) a recursive case — the self-call with a simpler or smaller argument, guaranteed to move toward the base case; call stack mechanics: each method call pushes a new stack frame holding its own local variables and return address; frames accumulate during the descent phase and unwind (pop) during the return phase — tracing this process step-by-step is the central AP CSA Unit 10 skill; classic examples and their traces — factorial: int factorial(int n) { if (n == 0) return 1; return n * factorial(n-1); } — factorial(4) descends 4→3→2→1→0(base), then unwinds returning 1→2→6→24; Fibonacci: int fib(int n) { if (n <= 1) return n; return fib(n-1) + fib(n-2); } — produces an exponential call tree (fib(5) makes 15 total calls); power: int power(int base, int exp) { if (exp == 0) return 1; return base * power(base, exp-1); }; sum of int array: int sum(int[] arr, int i) { if (i == arr.length) return 0; return arr[i] + sum(arr, i+1); }; String reversal: if (s.isEmpty()) return s; return reverse(s.substring(1)) + s.charAt(0); recursive binary search: if lo > hi return -1; compute mid = (lo+hi)/2; if arr[mid] == target return mid; recurse left if target < arr[mid], right otherwise — O(log n) stack frames; recursive vs iterative tradeoffs: any iterative algorithm can be rewritten recursively and vice versa; recursion is natural for divide-and-conquer and tree/graph problems but each call incurs function-call overhead and uses stack memory proportional to call depth; iterative versions use O(1) extra space; AP CSA exam question types — (1) trace: given a call like mystery(5), hand-execute each call and return in order and state the final return value; (2) count: how many times is the recursive method called for a given argument (fib(5) = 15 total calls); (3) identify: circle the base case and the recursive case in a provided method; (4) predict output: for print-side-effect methods, state what is printed in order including the descent and unwind phases; (5) write: implement a recursive method satisfying a written specification, ensuring the base case is reachable and the recursive argument strictly reduces toward it; a 4–6 question AP CSA Unit 10 chapter previously a bare stub",
