          "Creative development and program design (Big Idea 1 — CRD) — program design process: iterative and incremental development (design → prototype → test → evaluate → refine; each cycle improves the product using new feedback); program function vs program purpose: function describes what a program does technically (what inputs it accepts, what output it produces, what operations it performs) while purpose describes WHY it was created (the problem it solves, the need it meets, the audience it serves) — AP CSP exam explicitly tests whether you can distinguish the two; decomposition: breaking a complex problem into smaller, independently solvable sub-problems that can be recombined — reduces cognitive load and enables parallel development by a team; abstraction: hiding implementation details behind a simpler interface so a caller does not need to know how something works, only what it does (a procedure name hides its internal steps; a variable name hides its memory address; a list hides the underlying array mechanics); procedural abstraction: defining a named PROCEDURE with parameters that can be called repeatedly with different arguments — promotes code reuse, readability, and easier debugging; AP pseudocode conventions (used on the exam): PROCEDURE name(param1, param2) { block }, assignment uses ← (e.g., score ← score + 10), conditional: IF condition { } ELSE { }, iteration: REPEAT n TIMES { } and REPEAT UNTIL condition { }, list operations: INSERT(list, i, val), APPEND(list, val), REMOVE(list, i), LENGTH(list), index lists with list[i] (1-based in AP pseudocode); DISPLAY(expression) for output; flowcharts: oval = start/end, rectangle = process or assignment, diamond = decision (True/False branches exit different sides), parallelogram = input/output — used to communicate algorithm logic without code syntax; documentation: meaningful variable names describe the stored data (numStudents not n, isLoggedIn not flag), comments explain WHY non-obvious decisions were made (not WHAT the code does — that should be clear from the names), purpose statement at the top of a program; testing and debugging: test cases cover normal inputs (typical use), boundary/edge cases (empty list, maximum value, zero, negative numbers), and failure cases (invalid type, null input); hand-tracing — execute an algorithm step-by-step on paper with sample values to predict output before running; debugging strategies: DISPLAY statements to inspect variable values at each step, isolate the failing sub-problem by testing smaller parts independently, fix one issue at a time and retest; collaboration: pair programming (driver writes code, navigator reviews line-by-line in real time, then swap), asynchronous code review (reviewer reads and leaves comments, author addresses them), dividing a project into independent modules reduces merge conflicts; including diverse perspectives in design catches unstated assumptions and biases, leading to more usable and equitable programs; version control (e.g. Git): commits record snapshots, branches allow parallel development, pull requests enable peer review before merging; event-driven programming: programs respond to events (mouse click, key press, network response) by calling registered event-handler procedures instead of running top-to-bottom — most interactive applications (games, web apps, mobile apps) are event-driven; a core AP CSP Big Idea 1 (CRD — Creative Development) topic explicitly assessed in the AP CSP Create Performance Task (students design and code an original program with a video, written response, and code segment explaining abstraction and algorithm) and in multiple-choice questions on program design, abstraction, and iteration — previously absent from the AP CSP pack",
